  task_fidelity: {
    verdict: "pass",
    evidence: "Affected-only F3 update after viewing the source 317 prose and repaired view before keys. The four exact composed expressions and binomial/trinomial shape are unchanged. C-I now gives (a+5)(a^2+a+43), versus (a+5)(a^2+13a+43) in A/B/D; the full correct a-answer is no longer unanimous and the missing 12a creates a real mathematical distinction. Historical F3's specific copy-only invariant row is resolved. A/B still differ only on III, so I/II/IV cannot select A over B and the historical F2 c repair survives. Complete-list recognition still does not establish independent written expansion/factor production or separate mastery of every subpart; C can be rejected using II or III without judging I. Those declared recognition limits remain, and ordinary elimination is allowed.",
  },
  redundancy: {
    verdict: "pass",
    evidence: "Only C-I changed: row I now has two distinct algebraic products, not four copies of the correct full answer. III continues to distinguish A/B and C's inherited cross-term error; deleting III makes A/B identical, so other-row elimination cannot bypass 317c. The changed I assertion is a genuine competing product, not a duplicated supplied answer. Inherited cross-package nonredundancy evidence is reused without rereviewing the other 18 questions; selection is not evidence of separately judging all rows.",
  },
  answer_leakage: {
    verdict: "pass",
    evidence: "The unchanged visible stem contains no internal key, truth labels or source numbers. C-I's competing coefficient a rather than 13a removes the specific unanimous full-factorization disclosure for 317a; the factor cannot be copied as an agreed result from all alternatives. The new rationale stays internal. A/B's unchanged sole-III contrast means neither C's new I error nor any other-row judgment identifies the correct complete list without distinguishing III. Valid elimination may avoid separately judging I, but that ordinary recognition loss is not the earlier unanimous-answer leakage and is honestly retained.",
  },
  mathematics: {
    verdict: "pass",
    independent_solution: "All 14 original independent source solutions, including 317, and the prior A/B/D and unchanged C-II/III/IV mathematics are inherited from RECOVERY1, not freshly solved. The inherited correct products are (a+5)(a^2+13a+43), (y+1)(y^2-7y+19), (x-1)(x^2+10x+37), (2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2). The new view-first F3 check derives C-I by omitting 12a from (a+6)^2 and independently disproves it at a=1: 270 versus 342; original minus candidate is 12a(a+5). C's appended derivation and misconception exactly generate this changed row. A remains entirely true, B false only in III, C false in I/II/III, D false only in IV. The complete true set is exactly {A}, unchanged and agreeing with every internal truth label. This is one changed-row algebra check plus inherited mathematics, not a new blind whole-source solution.",
    correct: ["A"],
    option_checks: optionChecks,
  },
};
const source317Coverage = {
  verdict: "pass",
  evidence: "Affected-only F3 update; source 317's original independent_solution is inherited verbatim. All four original composed expressions and required factor shapes remain in the complete-product question. C-I now has a genuinely false missing-12a alternative (a=1: 270 versus 342), so the full correct answer for a is no longer supplied unanimously. A/B remain identical in I/II/IV and differ only in III, preserving the resolved historical F2 distinction; the other rows cannot choose A over B. F3's specific invariant supplied-answer loss is resolved for this new snapshot, not retroactively for RECOVERY1. The unchanged limitations and omitted_tasks retain loss of independently producing four products, and unit_reason/reason retain lack of separate-mastery evidence. C may still be eliminated via II or III without judging I; ordinary complete-table recognition remains within the contract, not independent production or proof of all-row mastery.",
